}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/292b2933-7707-5049-8262-56cf7fbbb1ae', 'word_count': 947, 'uri': 'nyt://article/292b2933-7707-5049-8262-56cf7fbbb1ae'}, {'abstract': 'The authorities have told the popular service Toutiao to clean up salacious material, illustrating the balance required to meet China’s voracious demand.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/02/business/china-toutiao-censorship.html', 'snippet': 'The authorities have told the popular service Toutiao to clean up salacious material, illustrating the balance required to meet China’s voracious demand.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG — One of the world’s most valuable start-ups got that way by using artificial intelligence to satisfy Chinese internet users’ voracious appetite for news and entertainment. Every day, its smartphone app feeds 120 million people personalized streams of buzzy news stories, videos of dogs frolicking in snow, GIFs of traffic mishaps and listicles such as “The World’s Ugliest Celebrities.”', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/03/world/03Toutiao/merlin_131797532_66fb05ff-cf15-498b-8f0f-de8f476f581a-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/03/world/03Toutiao/merlin_131797532_66fb05ff-cf15-498b-8f0f-de8f476f581a-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/03/world/03Toutiao/merlin_131797532_66fb05ff-cf15-498b-8f0f-de8f476f581a-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/03/world/03Toutiao/merlin_131797532_66fb05ff-cf15-498b-8f0