nts.html', 'snippet': 'The silence from chefs and owners has been deafening, but they need to admit and address the sexual predation that seems endemic to the business.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Another week, another chef down. This time it was Charlie Hallowell, who just before the end of the year took leave from daily operations of the three restaurants he owns in Oakland, Calif. Seventeen employees told Tara Duggan of The San Francisco Chronicle that Mr. Hallowell’s sexual harassment and verbal abuse were so routine that they stopped seeming remarkable.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/03/dining/03Harass2/03Harass2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/03/dining/03Harass2/03Harass2-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/03/dining/03Harass2/03Harass2-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/03/dining/03Harass2/03Harass2-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/03/dining/03Harass2/03Harass2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/01/03/dining/03Harass2/03Harass2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/03/dining/03Harass2/03Harass2-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Scandals Keep Breaking, but Restaurateurs Have Yet to Own Up', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Harassment Issues Need An Answer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sexual Harassment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': '#MeToo Movement', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chefs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Batali, Mario', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Besh, John (1968- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-01-02T16:55:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pete Wells', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pete', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wells', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b80a0a7b-8806-56b9-afaa-12baf9835f79', 'word_count': 1511, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b80a0a7b-8806-56b9-afaa-12baf9835f79'}, {'abstract': 'While the Alabama machine keeps chugging, there’s something charming about this Bulldogs team.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/02/sports/football/alabama-georgia-college-football-championship.html', 'snippet': 'While the Alabama machine keeps chugging, there’s something charming about this Bulldogs team.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEW ORLEANS — This started as a Nick Saban column, an assessment of the best college football coach in the game and perhaps the best coach in all of American sports. His Alabama team punished the top-ranked Clemson Tigers, 24-6, in the Sugar Bowl late Monday, sending the Tide to the national championship  game for the third time in four years and offering Saban a chance at his sixth championship.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/03/sports/ncaafootball/03onsugar1/merlin_131794970_43e40cdd-4551-48b0-afa3-fa0f68ff45c9-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/03/sports/ncaafootball/03onsugar1/merlin_131794970_43e40cdd-4551-48b0-afa3-fa0f68ff45c9-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/03/sports/ncaafootball/03onsugar1/merlin_131794970_43e40cdd-4551-48b0-afa3-fa0f68ff45c9-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/03/sports/ncaafootball/03onsugar1/merlin_131794970_43e40cdd-4551-48b0-afa3-fa0f68ff45c9-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/03/sports/ncaafootball/03onsugar1/merlin_131794970_43e40cdd-4551-48b0-afa3-fa0f68ff45c9-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/01/03/sports/ncaafootball/03onsugar1/merlin_131794970_43e40cdd-4551-48b0-afa3-fa0f68ff45c9-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/03/sports/ncaafootball/03onsugar1/merlin_131794970_43e40cdd-4551-48b0-afa3-fa0f68ff45c9-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Saban’s Great. We Get It. But Can We Pause to Embrace Georgia?', 'kicker': 'On College Football', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Saban Is an All-Time Great, But How ’Bout Them Dawgs?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football (College)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Alabama', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Georgia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Saban, Nick', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coaches and Managers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-01-02T16:57:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'College Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Drape', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Drape', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/076cecee-c561-5bd0-9f11-4c9765372642', 'word_count': 961, 'uri': 'nyt://article/076cecee-c561-5bd0-9f11-4c9765372642'}, {'abstract': 'A selection of books published this week; plus, a peek at what our colleagues around the newsroom are reading.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/02/books/review/new-noteworthy-rachel-dry.html', 'snippet': 'A selection of books published this week; plus, a peek at what our colleagues around the newsroom are reading.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE MAN WHO MADE THE MOVIES By Vanda Krefft. (Harper, $40.) Krefft has written the first major biography of William Fox, the movie mogul whose life story is the archetypical rags-to-riches tale — a boy who worked in a sweatshop on the Lower East Side eventually creates an entertainment empire. PHONE By Will Self. (Grove Press, $27.) The final novel in Self’s massive Umbrella Trilogy exploring technology and psychopathology, this book is set in London and Iraq and tells the story of two men, a psychiatrist losing his own mind and a mysterious MI-6 agent. MY TWENTIETH CENTURY EVENING AND OTHER SMALL BREAKTHROUGHS: THE NOBEL LECTURE By Kazuo Ishiguro. (Knopf, $16.95.) This is the lecture that the most recent Nobel laureate gave in Sweden in early December, looking at his own evolution as a writer and his thoughts on what a new generation of authors must do to keep literature relevant to our lives. IN DAYS TO COME By Avraham Burg. (Nation Books, $28.) The former speaker of Israel’s Knesset gives his own take on his country’s history and the quagmire it now finds itself in as Zionism and Jewish identity evolve to meet the new realities of the 21st century. TELL ME MORE By Kelly Corrigan. (Random House, $26.) Corrigan unpacks 12 essential phrases, from “I don’t know” to “I love you,” that, as she puts it, “turn the wheel of life.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/07/books/review/07newnoteworthy/07newnoteworthy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 303, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/07/books/review/07newnoteworthy/07newnoteworthy-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 303}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/07/books/review/07newnoteworthy/07newnoteworthy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 518, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/07/books/review/07newnoteworthy/07newnoteworthy-superJumbo.jpg'