 'subject', 'value': 'Solar System', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-01-02T17:51:34+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas St. Fleur', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'St', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/21075f9e-7dad-5282-83cc-732f33cd44a7', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/21075f9e-7dad-5282-83cc-732f33cd44a7'}, {'abstract': 'The uptown French classic returns, egg sandwiches served all day from the Tao Group, and more restaurant news.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/02/dining/la-goulue-nyc-restaurant-news.html', 'snippet': 'The uptown French classic returns, egg sandwiches served all day from the Tao Group, and more restaurant news.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LA GOULUE Even the phone number is the same at this serendipitous reincarnation of the French classic on the Upper East Side. Since the restaurant on Madison Avenue closed eight years ago, Jean Denoyer, who created the restaurant and is an owner, kept all the fixtures, nut-brown wood paneling, chairs and even the front doors in storage. He found a new space that has the approximate dimensions of the old one, including a nook for the tiny bar. The only major change is that, in the rear, there’s a spacious skylit room with stained glass, for additional seating and private parties. With Craig Pogson, a partner, Mr. Denoyer has seen to it that the chef and a partner, Antoine Camin, has maintained the menu with its steak tartare, tuna tartare, Burgundy snails, onion soup, sole meunière, roast chicken, steak au poivre vert, profiteroles and tarte Tatin. The wine list is still largely French. (Opens Monday): 29 East 61st Street, 212-988-8169, lagouluerestaurant.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': '