', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3e5f6c11-e2ae-573c-af05-6d49088e957f', 'word_count': 694, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3e5f6c11-e2ae-573c-af05-6d49088e957f'}, {'abstract': 'The artwork “Champ,” a white neon outline of a uterus with fiberglass boxing gloves, is to go on display there on Feb. 27.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/02/arts/design/hollywood-zoe-buckman-champ-women.html', 'snippet': 'The artwork “Champ,” a white neon outline of a uterus with fiberglass boxing gloves, is to go on display there on Feb. 27.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the corner of Sunset Boulevard and Sweetzer Avenue in front of The Standard in West Hollywood beginning on Feb. 27, a monumental white neon outline of a uterus — with fiberglass boxing gloves in place of the ovaries — will slowly rotate at a height of 43 feet. Well-positioned to be adopted as the logo of the #MeToo movement, this declarative image of female empowerment is the first public art project by Zoe Buckman, a 32-year-old British artist based in New York. Titled “Champ,” the kinetic sculpture was commissioned by the Art Production Fund and will remain on view for a year.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/03/arts/design/03buckman-item/03buckman-item-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 418, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/03/arts/design/03buckman-item/03buckman-item-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 418}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/03/arts/design/03buckman-item/03buckman-item-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 713, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, '