, 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Orr Shtuhl', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Orr', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shtuhl', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/df2102e2-ef17-54f3-90fd-8e1108910b07', 'word_count': 877, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df2102e2-ef17-54f3-90fd-8e1108910b07'}, {'abstract': 'As the legal markets for marijuana spread, a small credit union is solving a big problem: what to do with all the cash.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/04/magazine/where-pot-entrepreneurs-go-when-the-banks-just-say-no.html', 'snippet': 'As the legal markets for marijuana spread, a small credit union is solving a big problem: what to do with all the cash.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The small burgundy satchel bore the hallmarks of rugged craftsmanship: top-grain leather, double stitching on the straps. It was wider at the bottom, sort of like an inverted funnel, and two buckles secured the flap. Babak Behzadzadeh knew exactly how he might use the bag when he saw it hanging in a shop in Playa del Carmen, Mexico: It could be his bank.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '46', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/07/magazine/07mag-pot3/07mag-07pot-t_CA2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/07/magazine/07mag-pot3/07mag-07pot-t_CA2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/07/magazine/07mag-pot3/07mag-07pot-t_CA2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/07/magazine/07mag-pot3/07mag-07pot-t_CA2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/07/magazine/07mag-pot3/07mag-pot3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/01/07/magazine/07mag-pot3/07mag-pot3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/07/magazine/07mag-pot3/07mag-pot3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Where Pot Entrepreneurs Go When the Banks Just Say No', 'kicker': 'Feature', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'High Finance', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marijuana', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Money Laundering', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal-State Relations (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Checks and Checking Accounts', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automated Teller Machines', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medical Marijuana', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black Markets', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-01-04T10:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robb Mandelbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mandelbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d444b5cf-b752-5c41-8f3b-66c618c42871', 'word_count': 5984, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d444b5cf-b752-5c41-8f3b-66c618c42871'}, {'abstract': 'A dish that revives the familiar winter green.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/04/magazine/seeing-kale-with-new-eyes.html', 'snippet': 'A dish that revives the familiar winter green.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When people want to communicate that a food has the potential to take off — to flood restaurant menus and recipe blogs, to drive grocery sales and anchor lines of packaged health foods — they’ll often insist that it’s “the next kale.” It probably isn’t; kale’s triumph in the United States is exceptional. In a recent article for the online magazine Taste, the writer Mari Uyehara traced kale’s kaleidoscopic success — from relative obscurity as an Italian cooking green to chain-restaurant salads to the diets of millions of Americans — back to the fall of 2007.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/07/magazine/07mag-eat1-promo/07mag-07eat-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/07/magazine/07mag-eat1-promo/07mag-07eat-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/07/magazine/07mag-eat1-promo/07mag-07eat-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/07/magazine/07mag-eat1-promo/07mag-07eat-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy':