tps://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/04/technology/meltdown-spectre-questions.html', 'snippet': 'Hackers can exploit two major security flaws in microprocessors running virtually all machines on Earth. What do you do now?', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Wednesday, a group of security experts revealed two security flaws that affect nearly all microprocessors, the digital brains of the world’s computers. These flaws, called Meltdown and Spectre, could allow hackers to lift passwords, photos, documents and other data from smartphones, PCs and the cloud computing services that many businesses rely on.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/05/business/05CHIP/05CHIPFLAWSQANDA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/05/business/05CHIP/05CHIPFLAWSQANDA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/05/business/05CHIP/05CHIPFLAWSQANDA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/05/business/05CHIP/05CHIPFLAWSQANDA1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/05/business/05CHIP/05CHIPFLAWSQANDA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/01/05/business/05CHIP/05CHIPFLAWSQANDA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/05/business/05CHIP/05CHIPFLAWSQANDA1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What You Need to Do Because of Flaws in Computer Chips', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Lowdown on the Flaws in Computer Chips', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Chips', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Security', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cloud Computing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Linux (Operating System)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cyberattacks and Hackers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Intel Corporation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Microsoft Corp', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mozilla Foundation', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-01-04T23:56:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cade Metz and Brian X. Chen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cade', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Metz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': 'X.', 'lastname': 'Chen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/76ba2c50-628e-58de-b3b1-e9f727b78b53', 'word_count': 1188, 'uri': 'nyt://article/76ba2c50-628e-58de-b3b1-e9f727b78b53'}, {'abstract': 'Sure, the technology poses risks. But the current approach to regulating it is a mistake.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/04/opinion/leave-artificial-intelligence.html', 'snippet': 'Sure, the technology poses risks. But the current approach to regulating it is a mistake.', 'lead_paragraph': 'December was a big month for advocates of regulating artificial intelligence. First, a bipartisan group of senators and representatives introduced the Future of A.I. Act, the first federal bill solely focused on A.I. It would create an advisory committee to make recommendations about A.I. — on topics including the technology’s effect on the American work force and strategies to protect the privacy rights of those it impacts. Then the New York City Council approved a first-of-its-kind bill that once signed into law will create a task force to examine its own use of automated decision systems, with the ultimate goal of making its use of algorithms fairer and more transparent.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/05/opinion/05burtWeb/05burtWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/05/opinion/05burtWeb/05burtWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/05/opinion/05burtWeb/05burtWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/05/opinion/05burtWeb/05burtWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/05/opinion/05burtWeb/05burtWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/01/05/opinion/05burtWeb/05burtWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/05/opinion/05burtWeb/05burtWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Leave A.I. Alone', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Leave Artificial Intelligence Alone', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Artificial Intelligence', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-01-04T23:57:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Burt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/51189358-5ef5-5206-9d47-dc95571b2b59', 'word_count': 828, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51189358-5ef5-5206-9d47-dc95571b2b59'}, {'abstract': 'Much of what the federal government does has scientific and technological underpinnings. Yet the president has still not appointed a science adviser.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/04/opinion/trump-disdain-science.html', 'snippet': 'Much of what the federal government does has scientific and technological underpinnings. Yet the president has still not appointed a science adviser.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After almost a year in office, President Trump has yet to name a science adviser and director of the White House Office of Science and Technology Policy. Since World War II, no American president has shown greater disdain for science — or more lack of awareness of its likely costs.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/06/opinion/05Riordan/05Riordan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 601, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/06/opinion/05Riordan/05Riordan-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 601}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/06/opinion/05Riordan/05Riordan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 798, 'width': 797, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/06/opinion/05Riordan/05Riordan-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 798, 'width': 797, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/06/opinion/05Riordan/05Riordan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/01/06/opinion/05Riordan/05Riordan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/06/opinion/05Riordan/05Riordan-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Trump’s Disdain for Science', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributors', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The President’s Disdain for Science', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Office of Science and Technology Policy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-01-05T00:04:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neal F. Lane and Michael Riordan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neal', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Lane', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Riordan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/42ef6cc2-089f-59b3-a2a5-e31f05069208', 'word_count': 826, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42ef6cc2-089f-59b3-a2a5-e31f05069208'}, {'abstract': 'A journalist who loved to needle the Manhattan elite enrages the president with his insider account of the year he spent reporting from the West Wing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/04/business/media/michael-wolff-trump.html', 'snippet': 'A journalist who loved to needle the Manhattan elite enrages the president with his insider account of the year he spent reporting from the West Wing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Michael Wolff has, for years, been a prime piranha in the Manhattan media pond, using his caustic columns to tear into his lunchmates at Michael’s, the Midtown mogul canteen, and cutting a memorable figure at star-speckled dinner parties, clad in Charvet ties and shirts by the London haberdashery Browns.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/05/business/05WOLFF/05WOLFF-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 433, 'width': 600, 'subTy