rganization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/64c3af53-4e72-5e8f-8729-0dbb91c8b5d0', 'word_count': 1026, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64c3af53-4e72-5e8f-8729-0dbb91c8b5d0'}, {'abstract': 'In the movie, “I, Tonya,” the disgraced figure skater looks back on the 1994 Nancy Kerrigan scandal and her struggles to tell her side of the story.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/10/movies/tonya-harding-i-tonya-nancy-kerrigan-scandal.html', 'snippet': 'In the movie, “I, Tonya,” the disgraced figure skater looks back on the 1994 Nancy Kerrigan scandal and her struggles to tell her side of the story.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BATTLE GROUND, Wash. — Tonya Harding’s name isn’t Tonya Harding anymore. “My name is Tonya Price,” she said, when she saw me taking notes. In 2010, she had just returned home to Washington from Los Angeles, where she’d been doing another one of the odd jobs she’d collected over the years — color commentary on ill-advised video stunts for the TV show “World’s Dumbest.” She was still in her cute L.A. clothes, still made up, and she went out to Timbers, a local restaurant. She was having drinks with a friend when she spotted one Joe Price, a heating and air-conditioning worker, on the karaoke stage. He was singing “Great Balls of Fire.”', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/12/arts/14TONYAHARDING1/14TONYAHARDING1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/12/arts/14TONYAHARDING1/14TONYAHARDING1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 800}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/12/arts/14TONYAHARDING1/14TONYAHARDING1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/12/arts/14TONYAHARDING1/14TONYAHARDING1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1536, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/12/arts/14TONYAHARDING1/14TONYAHARDING1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/01/12/arts/14TONYAHARDING1/14TONYAHARDING1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/12/arts/14TONYAHARDING1/14TONYAHARDING1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tonya Harding Would Like Her Apology Now', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Still Nowhere to Run', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harding, Tonya', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Figure Skating', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kerrigan, Nancy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'I, Tonya (Movie)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-01-10T10:00:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Taffy Brodesser-Akner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Taffy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brodesser-Akner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/29b1613b-d4b0-546b-b995-cb4f77ce0aad', 'word_count': 4676, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29b1613b-d4b0-546b-b995-cb4f77ce0aad'}, {'abstract': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on whether a good deed is its own reward and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/10/magazine/should-i-accept-a-cash-reward-for-doing-the-right-thing.html', 'snippet': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on whether a good deed is its own reward and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My 12-year-old son and I found a cellphone in the back seat of a taxi. I called someone on the owner’s contact list who called him who then called me. We met on a convenient corner, and I gave him the phone. He was very appreciative and wanted to give us $40 to express his thanks. My son started to take it. I said: “Thank you, but no thank you. We didn’t do this for a reward.” Trying to explain integrity to my son, however, has been very difficult. He doesn’t see why we didn’t take money for a good deed. Even some of my friends said I should have taken the “reward.” What do you think? Name Withheld', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/14/magazine/14mag-ethicist/14mag-14ethicist-t_CA0-articleLarge.png', 'height': 931, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/14/magazine/14mag-ethicist/14mag-14ethicist-t_CA0-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 931}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/14/magazine/14mag-ethicist/14mag-14ethicist-t_CA0-jumbo.png', 'height': 1024, 'width': 660, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/14/magazine/14mag-ethicist/14mag-14ethicist-t_CA0-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1400, 'width': 902, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legac