llion Alabama Plant', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Factories and Manufacturing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mazda Motor Corp', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Toyota Motor Corp', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Huntsville (Ala)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Investments', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-01-10T21:03:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neal E. Boudette', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neal', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Boudette', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/824355a0-099c-5db6-9e6f-49aa0f01d0f8', 'word_count': 895, 'uri': 'nyt://article/824355a0-099c-5db6-9e6f-49aa0f01d0f8'}, {'abstract': 'Set in 1821, during the slavery era, the film is an austere story about white farmers and enslaved black men, women and children.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/10/movies/vazante-review-daniela-thomas.html', 'snippet': 'Set in 1821, during the slavery era, the film is an austere story about white farmers and enslaved black men, women and children.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Vazante” is an origin story written in bile and ornamented with reluctant beauty. It takes place in southeastern Brazil in 1821 — some six decades before the abolition of slavery — on a squalid farm deep in the mountains. There, a white drover turned farmer, Antonio (Adriano Carvalho), is trying to make a go of it on the backs of enslaved black men, women and children. He’s failed to cultivate the land, blaming it for his defeat, and seems equally terrible at everything else. Soon after the movie opens, his wife and baby die during labor, a tragedy that suggests that here life itself is doomed from the start.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/12/arts/12vazante/12vazante-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/12/arts/12vazante/12vazante-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/12/arts/12vazante/12vazante-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/12/arts/12vazante/12vazante-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/12/arts/12vazante/12vazante-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/01/12/arts/12vazante/12vazante-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/12/arts/12vazante/12vazante-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: ‘Vazante’ Revisits Brazil’s History in Black and White', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Brazil’s History in Black and White', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Vazante (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thomas, Daniela', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-01-10T21:08:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/182cf426-63ef-52d2-86a7-c17edaeb68db', 'word_count': 746, 'uri': 'nyt://article/182cf426-63ef-52d2-86a7-c17edaeb68db'}, {'abstract': 'With Congress yet to agree on a long-term plan to pay for the popular children’s health insurance program, parents start thinking about contingency plans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/10/health/chip-insurance-children-funding.html', 'snippet': 'With Congress yet to agree on a long-term plan to pay for the popular children’s health insurance program, parents start thinking about contingency plans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Children’s Health Insurance Program, better known as CHIP, covers nearly nine million children whose parents earn too much for Medicaid, but not enough to afford other coverage. But the program, which ran out of funding in September, is at a crisis point. Congress passed a stopgap spending bill late last month that was expected to keep CHIP running through March, but the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services said some states could run out of money as early as next week. We asked readers to tell us how they would be affected if their children lost CHIP coverage. Their stories have been condensed and edited for clarity.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/11/science/00CHIP-VOICES-ribiero/merlin_132025034_2ffe9890-cc8c-46e1-9639-67e950f827e3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 406, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/11/science/00CHIP-VOICES-ribiero/merlin_132025034_2ffe9890-cc8c-46e1-9639-67e950f827e3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 406}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/11/science/00CHIP-VOICES-ribiero/merlin_132025034_2ffe9890-cc8c-46e1-9639-67e950f827e3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 693, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/11/science/00CHIP-VOICES-ribiero/merlin_132025034_2ffe9890-cc8c-46e1-9639-67e950f827e3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1386, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/11/science/00CHIP-VOICES-ribiero/00CHIP-VOICES-ribiero-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/01/11/science/00CHIP-VOICES-ribiero/00CHIP-VOICES-ribiero-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/11/science/00CHIP-VOICES-ribiero/00CHIP-VOICES-ribiero-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What if CHIP Funds Run Out? Here’s What 6 Families Would Do', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What if CHIP Funds Run Out?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': "State Children's Health Insurance Program (S-CHIP)", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-01-10T21:10:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Fahima Haque', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Fahima', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haque', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/080bb0ac-fe0a-5074-a952-d131e626704b', 'word_count': 1578, 'uri': 'nyt://article/080bb0ac-fe0a-5074-a952-d131e626704b'}, {'abstract': '“In Between,” the new film by Maysaloun Hamoud, is the latest in a long line of feminist films by female Arab directors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/10/opinion/women-directors-middle-east.html', 'snippet': '“In Between,” the new film by Maysaloun Hamoud, is the latest in a long line of feminist films by female Arab directors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hailed as a fresh, frank look at the lives of young Palestinian women, Maysaloun Hamoud’s debut film, “In Between,” follows three female roommates who share an apartment in Tel Aviv. There, they participate, to varying degrees, in the debauchery on offer in Israel’s cultural capital: dancing, drinking, smoking, taking drugs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/13/opinion/10asfourweb/merlin_131702723_934a19ad-fd2f-48cc-9933-f55e5cc7e835-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/13/opinion/10asfourweb/merlin_131702723_934a19