'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/22e0de62-816e-5060-a775-46d3249abe54', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/22e0de62-816e-5060-a775-46d3249abe54'}, {'abstract': 'The civil rights leader was stabbed, honored and given a student pastoral assignment. He also led one of his biggest marches, against the war.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2018/01/11/what-martin-luther-king-jr-meant-to-new-york/', 'snippet': 'The civil rights leader was stabbed, honored and given a student pastoral assignment. He also led one of his biggest marches, against the war.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Rev. Dr. Martin Luther King Jr., though most associated with the South, had deep ties to New York City, dating back to the fall of 1950, when he served as a student pastor every other month at the First Baptist Church in East Elmhurst, Queens. The senior pastor there gave him a B for “pulpit ability,” knocking him for “aloofness.” That would change. The city almost saw the end of his career as well: at a 1958 book signing in Harlem, a woman named Izola Ware Curry stabbed him in the chest with a letter opener, convinced that Dr. King and others in the movement were plotting against her (she was committed to a hospital for the criminally insane). Dr. King remembered the incident in his “I Have Been to the Mountaintop” speech a day before his death, noting that if he had sneezed while the blade was in him, he would not have survived to see the gains of the movement he led. “I’m so happy that I didn’t sneeze,” he said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Martin Luther King Jr. Meant to New York', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil Rights Movement (1954-68)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'King, Martin Luther Jr', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-01-11T22:10:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Leland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/889f4c2b-76ab-5d65-a002-5f3fb59e864c', 'word_count': 374, 'uri': 'nyt://article/889f4c2b-76ab-5d65-a002-5f3fb59e864c'}, {'abstract': 'Joel Seligman resigned hours before the release of a report clearing his administration of charges of cover-up and retaliation in a sexual harassment case.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/11/nyregion/exonerated-university-president-resigns.html', 'snippet': 'Joel Seligman resigned hours before the release of a report clearing his administration of charges of cover-up and retaliation in a sexual harassment case.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The president of the University of Rochester stepped down on Thursday, just hours before an outside investigation cleared him and his administration of charges that they had covered up sexual harassment by a prominent professor and punished those who spoke out against him.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/12/nyregion/12rochester/merlin_132137873_3155250f-717f-467d-957a-a7e207a9af9e-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 403, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/12/nyregion/12rochester/merlin_132137873_3155250f-717f-467d-957a-a7e207a9af9e-articleLar