None, 'lastname': 'Bush', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/4eb3ea10-3a6a-5cb6-819b-cef7a66384ec', 'word_count': 784, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4eb3ea10-3a6a-5cb6-819b-cef7a66384ec'}, {'abstract': 'Suggested reading from critics and editors at The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/11/books/review/11-new-books-we-recommend-this-week.html', 'snippet': 'Suggested reading from critics and editors at The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Our list this week starts off with two very different debut novels. Hermione Hoby’s “Neon in Daylight” borrows its title from the poet Frank O’Hara, and recalls prior novels about ambivalent women by writers like Joan Didion and Zadie Smith. A. J. Finn’s “The Woman in the Window,” already a best seller, is packed with Hitchcock references and plot twists. Other suggestions include a sweeping history of the Israeli-Palestinian conflict, an account of how Istanbul has evolved through the centuries and James Lee Burke’s latest Dave Robicheaux mystery.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/14/books/review/14edcovers/14edcovers-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 294, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/14/books/review/14edcovers/14edcovers-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 294}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/14/books/review/14edcovers/14edcovers-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 409, 'width': 835, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/14/books/review/14edcovers/14edc