Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/19a2b8a1-c10f-5627-99e2-08f088cd8c57', 'word_count': 822, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19a2b8a1-c10f-5627-99e2-08f088cd8c57'}, {'abstract': 'A new book is a challenge to those who want to revive the liberal democratic order.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/11/opinion/how-democracies-perish.html', 'snippet': 'A new book is a challenge to those who want to revive the liberal democratic order.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Everybody agrees society is in a bad way, but what exactly is the main cause of the badness? Some people emphasize economic issues: The simultaneous concentration of wealth at the top and the stagnation in the middle has delegitimized the system. People like me emphasize cultural issues. If you have 60 years of radical individualism and ruthless meritocracy, you’re going to end up with a society that is atomized, distrustful and divided.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/12/opinion/12brooks-web/merlin_132154796_46bd43a1-b689-4d71-9d7a-7262fb52bc5c-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/12/opinion/12brooks-web/merlin_132154796_46bd43a1-b689-4d71-9d7a-7262fb52bc5c-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 402}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/12/opinion/12brooks-web/merlin_132154796_46bd43a1-b689-4d71-9d7a-7262fb52bc5c-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 685, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/12/opinion/12brooks-web/merlin_132154796_46bd43a1-b689-4d71-9d7a-7262fb52bc5c-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1371, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/12/opinion/12brooks-web/12brooks-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/01/12/opinion/12brooks-web/12brooks-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/12/opinion/12brooks-web/12brooks-web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Democracies Perish', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How Liberal Democracies Perish', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Democracy (Theory and Philosophy)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Berry, Wendell', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-01-12T01:31:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Brooks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/b75add52-7374-57da-8604-7b52440a81f5', 'word_count': 862, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b75add52-7374-57da-8604-7b52440a81f5'}, {'abstract': 'Walmart is raising hourly wages by $300 million, just a sliver of what it could save through tax cuts. But it’s a wonder Walmart can get away with so little.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/11/business/dealbook/walmart-wage-increase.html', 'snippet': 'Walmart is raising hourly wages by $300 million, just a sliver of what it could save through tax cuts. But it’s a wonder Walmart can get away with so little.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Walmart’s pay increase, purportedly inspired by the recent cut in the corporate tax rate, is a little less than real largess. The retailer’s extra spending on hourly wages is just a sliver of what it could save in taxes, though — and a tight labor market is likely to have been a more potent driver.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/12/business/12DB-VIEWSWALMART1/merlin_132143729_b8741804-2bc4-4970-a6a1-be8b5c3657b8-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/12/business/12DB-VIEWSWALMART1/merlin_132143729_b8741804-2bc4-4970-a6a1-be8b5c3657b8-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/12/business/12DB-VIEWSWALMART1/merlin_132143729_b8741804-2bc4-4970-a6a1-be8b5c3657b8-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/12/business/12DB-VIEWSWALMART1/merlin_132143729_b8741804-2bc4-4970-a6a1-be8b5c3657b8-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/12/business/12DB-VIEWSWALMART1/12DB-VIEWSWALMART1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/01/12/business/12DB-VIEWSWALMART1/1