 lives, has a sordid history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you want to know how reading saves the lives of people behind bars, listen to the poet and lawyer Reginald Dwayne Betts. At 16, in 1996, Mr. Betts was sentenced to eight years for a carjacking. Books, he has said, “became magic.” Reading became the way that he “learned about what it means to be human.” After being released from prison, Mr. Betts got his bachelor’s degree from the University of Maryland, received a Master of Fine Arts from Warren Wilson College and graduated from Yale Law School.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/11/opinion/12mzezewaweb/merlin_26976221_81e8a9fc-e691-4b11-9b6a-1a058df95ff0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/11/opinion/12mzezewaweb/merlin_26976221_81e8a9fc-e691-4b11-9b6a-1a058df95ff0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/11/opinion/12mzezewaweb/merlin_26976221_81e8a9fc-e691-4b11-9b6a-1a058df95ff0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/11/opinion/12mzezewaweb/merlin_26976221_81e8a9fc-e691-4b11-9b6a-1a058df95ff0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/11/opinion/12mzezewaweb/11mzezewaweb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/01/11/opinion/12mzezewaweb/11mzezewaweb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwi