9-92ac-44ed7f857cda'}, {'abstract': 'Should bird watchers protect these furtive creatures by keeping their locations secret?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/12/opinion/sunday/owl-watching.html', 'snippet': 'Should bird watchers protect these furtive creatures by keeping their locations secret?', 'lead_paragraph': 'ANNAPOLIS, Md. — Owls tend to be secretive. While there are a few American species that enjoy the daylight hours, most are nocturnal and spend their days behind thick greenery or uncannily blending into the bark of the trees they nestle against. Once they’ve found a secure place to snooze, they are likely to return to that spot daily, but even if you find evidence of their presence — scat and regurgitated pellets — good luck seeing the clandestine culprits.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/14/sunday-review/14COMET-slide-JG9V/14COMET-slide-JG9V-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/14/sunday-review/14COMET-slide-JG9V/14COMET-slide-JG9V-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/14/sunday-review/14COMET-slide-JG9V/14COMET-slide-JG9V-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/14/sunday-review/14COMET-slide-JG9V/14COMET-slide-JG9V-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/14/sunday-review/14COMET-slide-JG9V/14COMET-slide-JG9V-