', '_id': 'nyt://article/384d15ba-2451-5a03-8c4e-3c875a12b71e', 'word_count': 2195, 'uri': 'nyt://article/384d15ba-2451-5a03-8c4e-3c875a12b71e'}, {'abstract': 'On the northwest coast of Washington State, a community has chosen to live off the grid, some of them for the simplicity and saved money, some for safety.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/13/style/washington-olympic-peninsula-queer-intentional-community.html', 'snippet': 'On the northwest coast of Washington State, a community has chosen to live off the grid, some of them for the simplicity and saved money, some for safety.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OLYMPIC PENINSULA, Wash. — Somewhere on the Olympic Peninsula, which extends from the northwest coast of Washington, a community has chosen to live independent of the public supply of water, electricity and other utilities on which most residents rely. Linked by a diffuse network of shared friends and land, they would be impossible to locate without insider knowledge. Dense forest obfuscates their dwellings — tiny houses, trailers, a landlocked houseboat — often accessible only by dirt roads or footpaths. ', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/14/fashion/14THELOOK1/14THELOOK1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 819, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/14/fashion/14THELOOK1/14THELOOK1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 819}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/14/fashion/14THELOOK1/14THELOOK1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 750, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/14/fashion/14THELOOK1/14THELOOK1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height':