e were the first words spoken by Franklin, addressing Charlie Brown as the latter stared glumly out to sea. And this is how Charles M. Schulz integrated his comic strip, “Peanuts,” on July 31, 1968. Franklin’s initial three-strip arc unfolded quietly and gently, with the boys building a sand castle together while chatting. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/14/opinion/sunday/14Kamp1/merlin_132146669_bfb070e9-7806-4e76-a092-d00a02197bbd-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 520, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/14/opinion/sunday/14Kamp1/merlin_132146669_bfb070e9-7806-4e76-a092-d00a02197bbd-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 520}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/14/opinion/sunday/14Kamp1/merlin_132146669_bfb070e9-7806-4e76-a092-d00a02197bbd-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 888, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/14/opinion/sunday/14Kamp1/merlin_132146669_bfb070e9-7806-4e76-a092-d00a02197bbd-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1301, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/14/opinion/sunday/14Kamp1/14Kamp1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/01/14/opinion/sunday/14Kamp1/14Kamp1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/14/opinion/sunday/14Kamp1/14Kamp1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Guess Who’s Coming to ‘Peanuts’', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Guess Who’s Coming to ‘Peanuts’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schulz, Charles M', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cartoons and Cartoonists', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil Rights Movement (1954-68)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'King, Martin Luther Jr', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-01-13T19:30:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Kamp', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kamp', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/c0f45ef3-461a-5c4f-9949-4093a312fb7e', 'word_count': 1177, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c0f45ef3-461a-5c4f-9949-4093a312fb7e'}, {'abstract': 'Efforts to make wild places “safe” threaten to turn them into extensions of urban environments.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/13/opinion/sunday/keep-our-mountains-free-and-dangerous.html', 'snippet': 'Efforts to make wild places “safe” threaten to turn them into extensions of urban environments.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Last August, after several accidents and deaths among climbers on Mont Blanc, Western Europe’s highest and most treacherous mountain, Jean-Marc Peillex, the mayor of the French town of St. Gervais-les-Bains, issued an order: Anyone attempting to climb the nearby Gouter route up the mountain must now have specified gear including a harness, rope and headlamp. Those who do not take these precautions are to be fined.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/14/opinion/sunday/14sanzaro/14sanzaro-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1019, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/14/opinion/sunday/14sanzaro/14sanzaro-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1019}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/14/opinion/sunday/14sanzaro/14sanzaro-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 603, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/14/opinion/sunday/14sanzaro/14sanzaro-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1206, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/14/opinion/sunday/14sanzaro/14sanzaro-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/01/14/opinion/sunday/14sanzaro/14sanzaro-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/14/opinion/sunday/14sanzaro/14sanzaro-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Keep Our Mountains Free. And Dangerous.', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Keep Our Mountains Free. And Dangerous.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mountains', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Athletics and Sports', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-01-13T19:30:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Francis Sanzaro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Francis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sanzaro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/6bc580b6-64eb-54f1-bb1f-cf623b2cddbb', 'word_count': 1251, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6bc580b6-64eb-54f1-bb1f-cf623b2cddbb'}, {'abstract': 'What to say to the newly bereaved instead of “I just don’t know what to say.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/13/sunday-review/how-to-speak-grief.html', 'snippet': 'What to say to the newly bereaved instead of “I just don’t know what to say.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Grief has a language all its own, but even the best therapists can get tongue-tied around the tricky stuff. Our grief vocabulary isn’t clinical, and you probably won’t find it in the self-help books that friends hand you after the memorial service. But this does come from some hard-earned experience: We both lost parents as young adults. Loss is messy, melancholic and often darkly hilarious. It also lingers forever. Here’s a glossary that takes all that into account. Use it well.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/14/opinion/14opart-monday/14opart-monday-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 375, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/14/opinion/14opart-monday/14opart-