caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/21/travel/21Spain-slide-U2O2/21Spain-slide-U2O2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/01/21/travel/21Spain-slide-U2O2/21Spain-slide-U2O2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/21/travel/21Spain-slide-U2O2/21Spain-slide-U2O2-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Spain, Searching for Ancestors, Dry Wine and Calm Seas', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Spanish Feast for the Senses', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Spain', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Barcelona (Spain)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Malaga (Spain)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-01-16T10:00:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Luisita Lopez Torregrosa', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Luisita', 'middlename': 'Lopez', 'lastname': 'Torregrosa', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a6479bf1-2985-5257-b64c-fea4d644b1f7', 'word_count': 2998, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a6479bf1-2985-5257-b64c-fea4d644b1f7'}, {'abstract': 'In an exclusive interview, the (former) novelist shares his thoughts on Trump, #MeToo and retirement.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/16/books/review/philip-roth-interview.html', 'snippet': 'In an exclusive interview, the (former) novelist shares his thoughts on Trump, #MeToo and retirement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the death of Richard Wilbur in October, Philip Roth became the longest-serving member in the literature department of the American Academy of Arts and Letters, that august Hall of Fame on Audubon Terrace in northern Manhattan, which is to the arts what Cooperstown is to baseball. He’s been a member so long he can recall when the academy included now all-but-forgotten figures like Malcolm Cowley and Glenway Wescott — white-haired luminaries from another era. Just recently Roth joined William Faulkner, Henry James and Jack London as one of very few Americans to be included in the French Pleiades editions (the model for our own Library of America), and the Italian publisher Mondadori is also bringing out his work in its Meridiani series of classic authors. All this late-life eminence — which also includes the Spanish Prince of Asturias Award in 2012 and being named a commander in the Légion d’Honneur of France in 2013 — seems both to gratify and to amuse him. “Just look at this,” he said to me last month, holding up the ornately bound Mondadori volume, as thick as a Bible and comprising titles like “Lamento di Portnoy” and “Zuckerman Scatenato.” “Who reads books like this?”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/21/books/review/21McGrath/21McGrath-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 840, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/21/books/review/21McGrath/21McGrath-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 840}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/21/books/review/21McGrath/21McGrath-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 732, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/21/books/review/21McGrath/21McGrath-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1463, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/21/books/review/21McGrath/21McGrath-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/01/21/books/review/21McGrath/21McGrath-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/21/books/review/21McGrath/21McGrath-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'No Longer Writing, Philip Roth Still Has Plenty to Say', 'kicker': 'Interview', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Philip Roth Is Still Here', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Library of America', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roth, Philip', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-01-16T10:00:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles McGrath', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGrath', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/e377cd7c-9a48-5ae2-9911-8511b20d8991', 'word_count': 2357, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e377cd7c-9a48-5ae2-9911-8511b20d8991'}, {'abstract': 'Yes, it’s driven by greed — but the mania for cryptocurrency could wind up building something much more important than wealth.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/16/magazine/beyond-the-bitcoin-bubble.html', 'snippet': 'Yes, it’s driven by greed — but the mania for cryptocurrency could wind up building something much more important than wealth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The sequence of words is meaningless: a random array strung together by an algorithm let loose in an English dictionary. What makes them valuable is that they’ve been generated exclusively for me, by a software tool called MetaMask. In the lingo of cryptography, they’re known as my seed phrase. They might read like an incoherent stream of consciousness, but these words can be transformed into a key that unlocks a digital bank account, or even an online identity. It just takes a few more steps.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/21/magazine/21mag-cryptocurrency1-copy/21mag-cryptocurrency1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 665, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/21/magazine/21mag-cryptocurrency1-copy/21mag-cryptocurrency1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 665}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/21/magazine/21mag-cryptocurrency1-copy/21mag-cryptocurrency1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 925, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/21/magazine/21mag-cryptocurrency1-copy/21mag-cryptocurrency1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1849, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit'