e column, we meet a murderous nanny, an abducted teenager, a little boy stranded in the high desert and a mad bomber.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When a character in a crime novel snaps and kills a child, it’s usually a mother stressed beyond endurance. In Leila Slimani’s unnerving cautionary tale, THE PERFECT NANNY (Penguin, paper, $16), subtly translated by Sam Taylor, we know from the outset that it’s a beloved and trusted nanny who murders the two children in her care. That’s pretty radical for a domestic thriller; but what’s more remarkable about this unconventional novel (which was awarded France’s prestigious Prix Goncourt) is the author’s intimate analysis of the special relationship between a mother and a nanny.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/21/books/review/21Crime/21Crime-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/21/books/review/21Crime/21Crime-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/21/books/review/21Crime/21Crime-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/21/books/review/21Crime/21Crime-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2000, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/21/books/review/21Crime/21Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/01/21/books/review/21Crime/21Crim