: 'PARK CITY, Utah — In 1993, the writer-director Leslie Harris brought her first film, “Just Another Girl on the I.R.T.,” to Sundance. Focusing on Chantel (Ariyan Johnson), a headstrong, smart Brooklyn teenager, it won a special jury prize and landed a distribution deal. “Just Another Girl” was a rare thing then: a film written, directed and co-produced by a black woman, with a young black woman at the center of its story. Turns out it’s still a rare thing now.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/22/movies/leslieharris2/leslieharris2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/22/movies/leslieharris2/leslieharris2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/22/movies/leslieharris2/leslieharris2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/22/movies/leslieharris2/leslieharris2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/22/movies/leslieharris2/leslieharris2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/01/22/movies/leslieharris2/leslieharris2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/22/movies/leslieharris2/leslieharris2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 't