erson': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barbaro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca833035-8d47-5688-bf68-511b6b791fe4', 'word_count': 455, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca833035-8d47-5688-bf68-511b6b791fe4'}, {'abstract': 'A teenage girl embarks on a videotaped crime spree to satisfy her voracious appetite for diner food and live-streamed provocation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/25/movies/like-me-review.html', 'snippet': 'A teenage girl embarks on a videotaped crime spree to satisfy her voracious appetite for diner food and live-streamed provocation.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Like Me” begins with a teenage girl, Kiya (Addison Timlin), pointing a camera and a gun at a fast-food clerk. As the view alternates between what Kiya’s camera captures and what is beyond her field of vision, it’s unclear which is the real weapon — camera or gun. Kiya takes no money. Her only profit comes from capturing a terrified stranger weeping and urinating on camera.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/26/arts/26likeme/26likeme-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 324, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/26/arts/26likeme/26likeme-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 324}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/26/arts/26likeme/26likeme-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 554, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/26/arts/26likeme/26likeme-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1080, 'width': 1998, 'subType': 'sup