rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-01-25T20:14:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Olympics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Victor Mather and Rebecca R. Ruiz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Victor', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mather', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Rebecca', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Ruiz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7030ca5-87ca-5e7d-af02-7aa0a33b5a64', 'word_count': 677, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7030ca5-87ca-5e7d-af02-7aa0a33b5a64'}, {'abstract': 'Don’t try to pigeonhole her. She was more than any one thing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/25/opinion/ursula-le-guin.html', 'snippet': 'Don’t try to pigeonhole her. She was more than any one thing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Four years ago, on a midsummer Sunday, I rang the doorbell of an unassuming Victorian perched on the north slope of the Forest Park neighborhood of Portland, Ore., and waited for Ursula Kroeber Le Guin to come to the door. I’d grown up with — and in no small part, because of — her writing, from “Earthsea” to “The Left Hand of Darkness” to “The Dispossessed” to “Lavinia,” and the moment felt appropriately otherworldly. Not everyone is lucky enough to find himself ringing the doorbell of one of his literary heroes, let alone with a decent chance of being let in, and I was somewhat dumbstruck at the privilege. My host, when she came to the door, was decidedly less solemn.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/28/opinion/28wray/merlin_132888185_9d567051-7728-448f-81bd-bb98120d6059-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType':