l', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mr. Trump, Get It Right on the Holocaust', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-01-25T23:55:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/00b0a51e-714e-53a1-9048-bbb6173e1ab8', 'word_count': 563, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00b0a51e-714e-53a1-9048-bbb6173e1ab8'}, {'abstract': 'She may wind up doing more to upend expectations about first ladies than any of her more political, progressive predecessors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/25/opinion/melania-trump-radical.html', 'snippet': 'She may wind up doing more to upend expectations about first ladies than any of her more political, progressive predecessors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the first anniversary of his inauguration, President Trump spent the day blasting Democrats for the government shutdown, suggesting that women marching in protest of his presidency were somehow celebrating it, and embroiled in allegations that he paid off a porn star to keep her quiet about their relationship. Melania Trump, meanwhile, commemorated the anniversary by tweeting a single photo of herself on Inauguration Day on the arm of a Marine. Her husband was nowhere in sight, and she did not mention his name. A few days later — on what happened to be the Trumps’ 13th wedding anniversary — she canceled her plans to accompany Mr. Trump to the World Economic Forum in Davos, Switzerland.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/28/opinion/sunday/28brower/merlin_132885455_fe38e64e-90f8-4e21-bd96-d33f30dc8591-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/28/opinion/sunday/28brower/merlin_132885455_fe38e64e-90f8-4e21-bd96-d33f30dc8591-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/28/opinion/sunday/28brower/merlin_132885455_fe38e64e-90f8-4e21-bd96-d33f30dc8591-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/28/opinion/sunday/28brower/merlin_132885455_fe38e64e-90f8-4e21-bd96-d33f30dc8591-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/28/opinion/sunday/28brower/28brower-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/01/28/opinion/sunday/28brower/28brower-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/28/opinion/sunday/28brower/28brower-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Quiet Radicalism of Melania Trump', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Quiet Radicalism of Melania Trump', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'First Ladies (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Melania', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-01-25T23:55:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Andersen Brower', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': 'Andersen', 'lastname': 'Brower', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/33a7e18f-481b-5254-9900-ebb3d285b74a', 'word_count': 834, 'uri': 'nyt://article/33a7e18f-481b-5254-9900-ebb3d285b74a'}, {'abstract': 'The most significant long-term threat to United States national security may be our political polarization.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/25/opinion/national-security-polarization.html', 'snippet': 'The most significant long-term threat to United States national security may be our political polarization.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is well documented that Americans are ever more divided: along party, ideological, socio-economic and cultural lines; by geographic, demographic, racial and religious differences. Our political polarization hampers our ability to tackle important national issues, whether immigration, infrastructure, timely budgeting or closing Guantánamo. The recent government shutdown and the looming threat of another underscore this problem.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/25/opinion/26Rice2-web/merlin_132726113_33b8428e-6a0a-4ccf-88e3-ad5b53a74547-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/25/opinion/26Rice2-web/merlin_132726113_33b8428e-6a0a-4ccf-88e3-