aly', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3634bb2f-b30e-58d5-8ca4-8b988f9526ad', 'word_count': 1333, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3634bb2f-b30e-58d5-8ca4-8b988f9526ad'}, {'abstract': 'Even his friends were surprised by the range of the legendary former Random House publisher Howard Kaminsky’s books, from highbrow novels to “The Art of the Deal.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/26/books/review/howard-kaminsky-library.html', 'snippet': 'Even his friends were surprised by the range of the legendary former Random House publisher Howard Kaminsky’s books, from highbrow novels to “The Art of the Deal.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some were alphabetical: Four Laurie Colwin novels shared a shelf with Evan S. Connell’s “Mr. Bridge” and Michael Chabon’s “The Amazing Adventures of Kavalier & Clay.” Other sections must have had a private logic: “The Furies,” by Fernanda Eberstadt, flanked by Bob Woodward’s “State of Denial,” and “On Truth,” by the philosopher Harry Frankfurt. The library of the legendary former Random House publisher Howard Kaminsky, who died last summer, contained books by friends and mysteries he wrote with his wife, Susan, under the pseudonym Brooks Stanwood. In a high, far corner: “The Art of the Deal,” by Donald Trump, which he came to regret publishing. We asked some of Kaminsky’s friends and colleagues to share their thoughts on his collection.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/28/books/review/28Kaminsky2/28Kaminsky2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/28/books/review/28Kaminsky2/28Kaminsky2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype'