orted', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jessica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver-Greenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6dcbb2f3-12eb-5671-a9d8-f26f953ff16e', 'word_count': 1416, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6dcbb2f3-12eb-5671-a9d8-f26f953ff16e'}, {'abstract': 'A set of red chalk arrows drawn on the back of the work suggested that its orientation had been 180 degrees off.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/28/arts/design/morris-louis-painting-jewish-museum.html', 'snippet': 'A set of red chalk arrows drawn on the back of the work suggested that its orientation had been 180 degrees off.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Star of David may look the same upside-down as it does right-side-up, but the same can’t be said of a painting by the American Color Field artist Morris Louis that implements the Jewish symbol, and for years has potentially been viewed in flipped-over form.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/27/arts/27louis-item2/merlin_132927977_35dde818-f688-4d04-a29d-e66709bb562f-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 722, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/27/arts/27louis-item2/merlin_132927977_35dde818-f688-4d04-a29d-e66709bb562f-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 722}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/27/arts/27louis-item2/merlin_132927977_35dde818-f688-4d04-a29d-e66709bb562f-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 852, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/27/arts/27louis-item2/merlin