5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-01-29T01:19:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Editorials', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/44d6f8f2-edc6-5547-ab40-0ad4657e1e3f', 'word_count': 571, 'uri': 'nyt://article/44d6f8f2-edc6-5547-ab40-0ad4657e1e3f'}, {'abstract': 'New rules and bureaucracy allow a range of medical care to be denied on the grounds of religious freedom.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/28/opinion/editorials/white-house-religious-freedom-doctors.html', 'snippet': 'New rules and bureaucracy allow a range of medical care to be denied on the grounds of religious freedom.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tamesha Means had been pregnant for just 18 weeks when her water broke in 2010. In pain, she rushed to a hospital near her house in Michigan. But because it was a Catholic health center, doctors there did not tell her that continuing her pregnancy could threaten her health and that abortion was her safest option. Instead they sent her home. They did so again when she returned the next day, bleeding, with painful contractions. They were preparing to send her home for a third time when she miscarried at the hospital.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/29/opinion/29mon2/29mon2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 722, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/29/opinion/29mon2/29mon2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 722}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/29/opinion/29mon2/29mon2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 852, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/29/opinion/29mon2/29mon2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1199, 'width': 997, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/29/opinion/29mon2/29mon2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/01/29/opinion/29mon2/29mon2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/29/opinion/29mon2/29mon2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The White House Puts the Bible Before the Hippocratic Oath', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'White House Puts a Bible in Doctors’ Offices', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ABORTION', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birth Control and Family Planning', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Religion', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicaid', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Health and Human Services Department', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Planned Parenthood Federation of America', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-01-29T01:23:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Editorials', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Ed