tion_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katie Kitamura', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kitamura', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d52f653-1c47-50d0-b488-6c0351d4c577', 'word_count': 1316, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d52f653-1c47-50d0-b488-6c0351d4c577'}, {'abstract': 'We have the “beach read” and the “airplane read,” but what about those books best suited for the subway?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/29/books/review/subway-reads.html', 'snippet': 'We have the “beach read” and the “airplane read,” but what about those books best suited for the subway?', 'lead_paragraph': 'When people talk about books, they often characterize either the genre (science fiction, romance) or the feeling the author strives to impart (a thriller is, presumably, thrilling). But there are, to my knowledge, only a handful of geographically specific kinds of reads. There’s the “beach read,” a phrase we all hear often come every summer season. There’s the notoriously disposable “airplane read.” And for getaways in the woods, you’ve got the “cottage” or “cabin read.” And that’s about it.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/04/books/review/04Sternbergh1/04Sternbergh1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/04/books/review/04Sternbergh1/04Sternbergh1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/04/books/review/04Sternbergh1/04Sternbergh1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name