Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bari Weiss', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bari', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weiss', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/8122c2b5-4810-5128-93e1-34a61e2e229a', 'word_count': 858, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8122c2b5-4810-5128-93e1-34a61e2e229a'}, {'abstract': 'In a wrongheaded effort to rewrite history, Poland’s Parliament is debating a bill to criminalize discussion of that nation’s role in the Holocaust.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/29/opinion/poland-holocaust-bill-parliament.html', 'snippet': 'In a wrongheaded effort to rewrite history, Poland’s Parliament is debating a bill to criminalize discussion of that nation’s role in the Holocaust.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is baffling why Poland’s nationalist-controlled Parliament would mark International Holocaust Remembrance Day — the anniversary of the liberation of Auschwitz-Birkenau, the largest Nazi concentration camp on Polish soil — with a needless, foolish and insulting draft bill that would penalize any suggestion of complicity by the Polish state or the Polish nation in the Nazi death machine.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/30/opinion/30tue1-web/merlin_132970313_825045a1-efe4-4a74-b1af-6d94077c68f8-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 413, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/30/opinion/30tue1-web/merlin_132970313_825045a1-efe4-4a74-b1af-6d94077c68f8-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 413}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/30/opinion/30tue1-web/merlin_132970313_825045a1-efe4-4a74-b1af-6d94077c68f8-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 705, 'width'