', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Bornstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bornstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/13a908b1-f834-562b-b393-26f371d1bfbf', 'word_count': 1912, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13a908b1-f834-562b-b393-26f371d1bfbf'}, {'abstract': 'Edward Lansdale understood the Vietnam War better than anyone and tried to point out the tragic mistakes. Too bad no one listened.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/30/opinion/american-tet-offensive-lansdale.html', 'snippet': 'Edward Lansdale understood the Vietnam War better than anyone and tried to point out the tragic mistakes. Too bad no one listened.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Tet offensive, which began 50 years ago today and is remembered as the turning point of the Vietnam War, caught Americans by surprise. One of the few who saw what was coming was Edward Lansdale, the legendary covert operative and retired Air Force general who had helped to create the state of South Vietnam after the French withdrew. He had returned to Saigon in 1965 as an official at the American Embassy, trying to use his close ties to the South Vietnamese to salvage something from a failing war effort.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/30/opinion/30Vietnam--boot-web/merlin_131832527_a0cc5c8a-1edd-4f07-aaee-8871a09ded32-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 407, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/30/opinion/30Vietnam--boot-web/merlin_131832527_a0cc5c8a-1edd-4f07-aaee-8871a09ded32-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 407}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/30/opinion/30Vietnam--boot-web/merlin_131832527_a0cc5c8a-1edd-4f07-aaee-8871a09ded32-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 695, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/30/opinion/30Vietnam--boot-web/merlin_131832527_a0cc5c8a-1edd-4f07-aaee-8871a09ded32-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1389, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/30/opinion/30Vietnam--boot-web/30Vietnam--boot-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/01/30/opinion/30Vietnam--boot-web/30Vietnam--boot-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/30/opinion/30Vietnam--boot-web/30Vietnam--boot-web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The American Who Predicted Tet', 'kicker': "Vietnam '67", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vietnam War', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 1968', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lansdale, Edward G', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vietnam', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-01-30T10:45:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Max Boot', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Max', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Boot', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 