 show.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Leni Riefenstahl, the Nazi filmmaker, desperately wanted her movies to be viewed as distinct from herself and her politics. They were, she insisted, artistic “cinéma vérité.” But in reality, they were propaganda — communicative devices with clear and biased political goals not subject to interpretation, and therefore rightfully categorized as something other than art.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/30/opinion/30Delistraty-web/merlin_115598687_eebea447-003c-4f11-9cbe-59957d7ca790-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/01/30/opinion/30Delistraty-web/merlin_115598687_eebea447-003c-4f11-9cbe-59957d7ca790-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/30/opinion/30Delistraty-web/merlin_115598687_eebea447-003c-4f11-9cbe-59957d7ca790-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/30/opinion/30Delistraty-web/merlin_115598687_eebea447-003c-4f11-9cbe-59957d7ca790-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/01/30/opinion/30Delistraty-web/30Delistraty-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/01/30/opinion/30Delistraty-web/30Delistraty-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image'