iewozik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1cc1224e-81e1-5196-8fca-fde3f5e66a5e', 'word_count': 1103, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1cc1224e-81e1-5196-8fca-fde3f5e66a5e'}, {'abstract': 'The geophysicist on climate-change deniers, optimism for the planet and women in STEM fields.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/31/magazine/robin-bell-doesnt-think-science-should-be-political.html', 'snippet': 'The geophysicist on climate-change deniers, optimism for the planet and women in STEM fields.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last year, you began your tenure as the president-elect of the American Geophysical Union, a global organization of earth and space scientists who work on ensuring a sustainable future. Do you feel as if we’re at a crossroads for climate change? Yes. We’ve gone from a place where scientists predicted change from invisible gases, to where we could visibly see the change in the polar region, to now seeing it on our front doorsteps from warmer days, shorter winters and rising sea levels. Our knowledge of our planet has made science political, but it’s not — it’s a public resource.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '82', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/04/magazine/04mag-talk-promo/04mag-talk-promo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/04/magazine/04mag-talk-promo/04mag-talk-promo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/04/magazine/04mag-talk-promo/04mag-talk-promo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumb