, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8fdef6f3-1521-5ec0-87d1-c88cfdd0fc1b', 'word_count': 442, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8fdef6f3-1521-5ec0-87d1-c88cfdd0fc1b'}, {'abstract': 'Even if he wanted to, he can’t unwatch his movies, our critic writes. The relationship between filmmaker and viewer is as complicated as the one between art and artist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/01/31/movies/woody-allen.html', 'snippet': 'Even if he wanted to, he can’t unwatch his movies, our critic writes. The relationship between filmmaker and viewer is as complicated as the one between art and artist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the morning of the Oscar nominations, I was chatting with a stranger about movies, as one does. The conversation turned to Woody Allen. “My son has seen all his movies, and he thinks he’s innocent,” she said. “I’ve seen all his movies, and I think he’s guilty,” I said. There was not much else to say.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/01/arts/01WOODY-ALLEN1/01WOODY-ALLEN1-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 311, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/02/01/arts/01WOODY-ALLEN1/01WOODY-ALLEN1-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 311}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/01/arts/01WOODY-ALLEN1/01WOODY-ALLEN1-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 531, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/01/arts/01WOODY-ALLEN1/01WOODY-ALLEN1-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 921, 'width': 1776, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/01/arts/01WOODY-ALLEN1/01WOODY-ALLEN1-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/02/01/arts/01WOODY-ALLEN1/01WOODY-ALLEN1-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/02/01/arts/01WOODY-ALLEN1/01WOODY-ALLEN1-thumbLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'My Woody Allen Problem', 'kicker': 'On Second Thought', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Coming to Terms With Woody Allen', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ALLEN, WOODY', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Farrow, Malone (Dylan)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Farrow, Mia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Previn, Soon-Yi', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Abuse and Neglect', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-01-31T16:51:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/09032f8d-77e4-5471-bd62-abd768af89dd', 'word_count': 1580, 'uri': 'nyt://article/09032f8d-77e4-5471-bd62-abd768af89dd'}, {'abstract': 'The director of “The Shape of